title: Pagination (Octane) Guide
---
NIFT_TANSTACK_DOCUMENT_V1
{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"examples","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Examples"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Want to skip to the implementation? Check out these Octane examples:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"../examples/pagination","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pagination"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"pagination-setup","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pagination Setup"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Here's how you set up your table to use pagination features. Adding the pagination feature enables the related APIs. If you use client-side pagination, also set up "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"paginatedRowModel"},{"type":"text","value":" after its feature, since row model slots are type-checked."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import {\n  useTable,\n  tableFeatures,\n  rowPaginationFeature,\n  createPaginatedRowModel,\n} from '@tanstack/octane-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  rowPaginationFeature,\n  paginatedRowModel: createPaginatedRowModel(), // if using client-side pagination\n  // manualPagination: true, // if using manual server-side pagination\n})\n\nconst table = useTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n})","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"pagination-octane-guide","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pagination (Octane) Guide"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack Table has great support for both client-side and server-side pagination. This guide will walk you through the different ways to implement pagination in your table."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"client-side-pagination","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Client-Side Pagination"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Using client-side pagination means that the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"data"},{"type":"text","value":" that you fetch will contain "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ALL"}]}]},{"type":"text","value":" of the rows for the table, and the table instance will handle pagination logic in the front-end."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":4,"id":"should-you-use-client-side-pagination","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Should You Use Client-Side Pagination?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Client-side pagination is usually the simplest option when the browser can fetch and retain the complete dataset. Use server-side pagination when the full dataset would be too expensive to query, transfer, or store in the browser."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Row count alone does not decide the boundary. See the "},{"type":"link","href":"../../../guide/client-side-vs-server-side","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Client-Side vs Server-Side Guide"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for the full decision framework, performance factors, and guidance for keeping filtering and sorting consistent with pagination."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":4,"id":"should-you-use-virtualization-instead","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Should You Use Virtualization Instead?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Virtualization (or windowing) reduces rendering work by mounting only the visible rows, but the virtualized data still exists in the browser. It can complement client-side or server-side pagination, but it does not replace server-side processing when the complete dataset is too large to load."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"See the "},{"type":"link","href":"../../../guide/client-side-vs-server-side#rendering-is-a-separate-decision","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Client-Side vs Server-Side Guide"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for that distinction, or "},{"type":"link","href":"https://tanstack.com/virtual/latest","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack Virtual"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for virtualization APIs."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":4,"id":"pagination-row-model","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pagination Row Model"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If you want to take advantage of the built-in client-side pagination in TanStack Table, add the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"rowPaginationFeature"},{"type":"text","value":" and the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"paginatedRowModel"},{"type":"text","value":" factory to your "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tableFeatures"},{"type":"text","value":" call:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import {\n  useTable,\n  tableFeatures,\n  rowPaginationFeature,\n  createPaginatedRowModel,\n} from '@tanstack/octane-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  rowPaginationFeature,\n  paginatedRowModel: createPaginatedRowModel(),\n})\n\nconst table = useTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n})","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"manual-server-side-pagination","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Manual Server-Side Pagination"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If you decide that you need to use server-side pagination, here is how you can implement it."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"No pagination row model is needed for server-side pagination, but if you have provided it for other tables that do need it in a shared component, you can still turn off the client-side pagination by setting the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"manualPagination"},{"type":"text","value":" option to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"true"},{"type":"text","value":". Setting the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"manualPagination"},{"type":"text","value":" option to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"true"},{"type":"text","value":" will tell the table instance to use the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.getPrePaginatedRowModel"},{"type":"text","value":" row model under the hood, and it will make the table instance assume that the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"data"},{"type":"text","value":" that you pass in is already paginated."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":4,"id":"page-count-and-row-count","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Page Count and Row Count"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The table instance will have no way of knowing how many rows/pages there are in total in your back-end unless you tell it. Provide either the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"rowCount"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pageCount"},{"type":"text","value":" table option to let the table instance know how many pages there are in total. If you provide a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"rowCount"},{"type":"text","value":", the table instance will calculate the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pageCount"},{"type":"text","value":" internally from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"rowCount"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pageSize"},{"type":"text","value":". Otherwise, you can directly provide the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pageCount"},{"type":"text","value":" if you already have it. If you don't know the page count, pass "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"-1"},{"type":"text","value":" for "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pageCount"},{"type":"text","value":". In that case, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getCanNextPage()"},{"type":"text","value":" returns "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"true"},{"type":"text","value":" because the table cannot detect the end, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getCanPreviousPage()"},{"type":"text","value":" depends on the current "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pageIndex"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getCanLastPage()"},{"type":"text","value":" returns "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"false"},{"type":"text","value":" because no finite last page is known."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import {\n  useTable,\n  tableFeatures,\n  rowPaginationFeature,\n} from '@tanstack/octane-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({ rowPaginationFeature }) // no paginatedRowModel for server-side pagination\n\nconst table = useTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n  manualPagination: true, // turn off client-side pagination\n  rowCount: dataQuery.data?.rowCount, // pass in the total row count so the table knows how many pages there are (pageCount calculated internally if not provided)\n  // pageCount: dataQuery.data?.pageCount, // alternatively directly pass in pageCount instead of rowCount\n})","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"callout","kind":"note","title":"Note","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Setting the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"manualPagination"},{"type":"text","value":" option to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"true"},{"type":"text","value":" will make the table instance assume that the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"data"},{"type":"text","value":" that you pass in is already paginated."}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"pagination-state","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pagination State"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Whether or not you are using client-side or manual server-side pagination, you can use the built-in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pagination"},{"type":"text","value":" state and APIs."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pagination"},{"type":"text","value":" state is an object that contains the following properties:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pageIndex"},{"type":"text","value":": The current page index (zero-based)."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pageSize"},{"type":"text","value":": The current page size."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For reactive reads that should re-render your UI, use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.state.pagination"},{"type":"text","value":". In event handlers, you can read the current snapshot with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.atoms.pagination.get()"},{"type":"text","value":", but this read does not subscribe the component to future changes."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If you need access to the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pagination"},{"type":"text","value":" state outside of the table (a server-side query key is the most common case), you can own the slice yourself. The recommended way in v9 is an external atom passed through the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"atoms"},{"type":"text","value":" table option. Atoms preserve fine-grained subscriptions, and the pagination value can be used in a query key without forcing the component that owns the table to re-render."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { useCreateAtom, useSelector } from '@tanstack/octane-store'\nimport {\n  useTable,\n  tableFeatures,\n  rowPaginationFeature,\n  createPaginatedRowModel,\n  type PaginationState,\n} from '@tanstack/octane-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  rowPaginationFeature,\n  paginatedRowModel: createPaginatedRowModel(),\n})\n\nconst paginationAtom = useCreateAtom<PaginationState>({\n  pageIndex: 0, // initial page index\n  pageSize: 10, // default page size\n})\n\n// subscribe to the atom wherever you need the value (e.g. for a query key)\nconst pagination = useSelector(paginationAtom)\n\nconst table = useTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n  atoms: {\n    pagination: paginationAtom, // table pagination APIs now update paginationAtom\n  },\n})","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Alternatively, the v8-style "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"state.pagination"},{"type":"text","value":" plus "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onPaginationChange"},{"type":"text","value":" pattern is still supported. It can be convenient for simple integrations or when migrating v8 code, but it is less fine-grained than external atoms. See the "},{"type":"link","href":"./table-state","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Table State Guide"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for a deeper comparison."}]},{"type":"code","value":"const [pagination, setPagination] = useState<PaginationState>({\n  pageIndex: 0, // initial page index\n  pageSize: 10, // default page size\n})\n\nconst table = useTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n  onPaginationChange: setPagination,\n  state: {\n    pagination,\n  },\n})","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Alternatively, if you have no need for managing the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pagination"},{"type":"text","value":" state in your own scope, but you need to set different initial values for the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pageIndex"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pageSize"},{"type":"text","value":", you can use the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"initialState"},{"type":"text","value":" option."}]},{"type":"code","value":"const table = useTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n  initialState: {\n    pagination: {\n      pageIndex: 2, // custom initial page index\n      pageSize: 25, // custom default page size\n    },\n  },\n})","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"callout","kind":"note","title":"Note","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Do NOT provide the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pagination"},{"type":"text","value":" slice in more than one of the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"atoms"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"state"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"initialState"},{"type":"text","value":" options. Controlled values ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"atoms"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"state"},{"type":"text","value":") will overwrite "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"initialState"},{"type":"text","value":". Only use one of them."}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"pagination-options","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pagination Options"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Besides the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"manualPagination"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pageCount"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"rowCount"},{"type":"text","value":" options which are useful for manual server-side pagination (and discussed "},{"type":"link","href":"#manual-server-side-pagination","children":[{"type":"text","value":"above"}]},{"type":"text","value":"), there is one other table option that is useful to understand."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":4,"id":"auto-reset-page-index","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Auto Reset Page Index"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"By default, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pageIndex"},{"type":"text","value":" is reset to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"0"},{"type":"text","value":" whenever the client-side row models recompute, such as when the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"data"},{"type":"text","value":" is updated, filters change, sorting changes, or grouping changes. This behavior is automatically disabled when "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"manualPagination"},{"type":"text","value":" is "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"true"},{"type":"text","value":", but it can be overridden by explicitly assigning a boolean value to the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"autoResetPageIndex"},{"type":"text","value":" table option. There is also a global "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"autoResetAll"},{"type":"text","value":" table option that disables (or enables) every auto-reset behavior at once."}]},{"type":"callout","kind":"note","title":"Note","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Automatic resets run only when an included client-side row model that triggers them recomputes. If a manual server-side table omits the filtered, sorted, grouped, or other relevant row model, changing that controlled state does not trigger a page-index reset, even when "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"autoResetPageIndex"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"autoResetAll"},{"type":"text","value":" is "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"true"},{"type":"text","value":". Reset "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pageIndex"},{"type":"text","value":" yourself in the corresponding change handler."}]}]},{"type":"code","value":"const table = useTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n  autoResetPageIndex: false, // turn off auto reset of pageIndex\n  // autoResetAll: false, // or turn off all auto resets at once\n})","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A common reason to set "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"autoResetPageIndex: false"},{"type":"text","value":" is editing data while viewing the table (for example, inline cell editing). Every edit updates "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"data"},{"type":"text","value":", which recomputes the row models and would otherwise snap the user back to the first page. Setting the option to a static "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"false"},{"type":"text","value":" keeps the current page when the row model recomputes. If you also use the expanding feature, pair it with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"autoResetExpanded: false"},{"type":"text","value":" so expanded rows do not collapse on edits."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Be aware, however, that if you turn off "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"autoResetPageIndex"},{"type":"text","value":", you may need to add some logic to handle resetting the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pageIndex"},{"type":"text","value":" yourself to avoid showing empty pages."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"pagination-apis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pagination APIs"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"There are several pagination table instance APIs that are useful for hooking up your pagination UI components."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":4,"id":"pagination-button-apis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pagination Button APIs"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getCanPreviousPage"},{"type":"text","value":": Useful for disabling the \"previous page\" button when on the first page."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getCanNextPage"},{"type":"text","value":": Useful for disabling the \"next page\" button when there are no more pages."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getCanLastPage"},{"type":"text","value":": Useful for disabling the \"last page\" button when no finite last page is known."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"previousPage"},{"type":"text","value":": Useful for going to the previous page. (Button click handler)"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"nextPage"},{"type":"text","value":": Useful for going to the next page. (Button click handler)"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"firstPage"},{"type":"text","value":": Useful for going to the first page. (Button click handler)"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"lastPage"},{"type":"text","value":": Useful for going to the last page. (Button click handler)"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"setPageIndex"},{"type":"text","value":": Useful for a \"go to page\" input."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"resetPageIndex"},{"type":"text","value":": Useful for resetting the table state to the original page index."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"setPageSize"},{"type":"text","value":": Useful for a \"page size\" input/select."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"resetPageSize"},{"type":"text","value":": Useful for resetting the table state to the original page size."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"setPagination"},{"type":"text","value":": Useful for setting all of the pagination state at once."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"resetPagination"},{"type":"text","value":": Useful for resetting the table state to the original pagination state."}]}]}]},{"type":"callout","kind":"note","title":"Note","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"These pagination APIs are available when using "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"rowPaginationFeature"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"code","value":"<Button\n  onClick={() => table.firstPage()}\n  disabled={!table.getCanPreviousPage()}\n>\n  {'<<'}\n</Button>\n<Button\n  onClick={() => table.previousPage()}\n  disabled={!table.getCanPreviousPage()}\n>\n  {'<'}\n</Button>\n<Button\n  onClick={() => table.nextPage()}\n  disabled={!table.getCanNextPage()}\n>\n  {'>'}\n</Button>\n<Button\n  onClick={() => table.lastPage()}\n  disabled={!table.getCanLastPage()}\n>\n  {'>>'}\n</Button>\n<select\n  value={table.state.pagination.pageSize}\n  onChange={e => {\n    table.setPageSize(Number(e.target.value))\n  }}\n>\n  {[10, 20, 30, 40, 50].map(pageSize => (\n    <option key={pageSize} value={pageSize}>\n      {pageSize}\n    </option>\n  ))}\n</select>","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":4,"id":"pagination-info-apis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pagination Info APIs"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getPageCount"},{"type":"text","value":": Useful for showing the total number of pages."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getRowCount"},{"type":"text","value":": Useful for showing the total number of rows."}]}]}]}],"headings":[{"id":"examples","text":"Examples","level":2},{"id":"pagination-setup","text":"Pagination Setup","level":3},{"id":"pagination-octane-guide","text":"Pagination (Octane) Guide","level":2},{"id":"client-side-pagination","text":"Client-Side Pagination","level":3},{"id":"should-you-use-client-side-pagination","text":"Should You Use Client-Side Pagination?","level":4},{"id":"should-you-use-virtualization-instead","text":"Should You Use Virtualization Instead?","level":4},{"id":"pagination-row-model","text":"Pagination Row Model","level":4},{"id":"manual-server-side-pagination","text":"Manual Server-Side Pagination","level":3},{"id":"page-count-and-row-count","text":"Page Count and Row Count","level":4},{"id":"pagination-state","text":"Pagination State","level":3},{"id":"pagination-options","text":"Pagination Options","level":3},{"id":"auto-reset-page-index","text":"Auto Reset Page Index","level":4},{"id":"pagination-apis","text":"Pagination APIs","level":3},{"id":"pagination-button-apis","text":"Pagination Button APIs","level":4},{"id":"pagination-info-apis","text":"Pagination Info APIs","level":4}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\n## Examples\n\nWant to skip to the implementation? Check out these Octane examples:\n\n- [Pagination](../examples/pagination)\n\n### Pagination Setup\n\nHere's how you set up your table to use pagination features. Adding the pagination feature enables the related APIs. If you use client-side pagination, also set up `paginatedRowModel` after its feature, since row model slots are type-checked.\n\n```tsx\nimport {\n  useTable,\n  tableFeatures,\n  rowPaginationFeature,\n  createPaginatedRowModel,\n} from '@tanstack/octane-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  rowPaginationFeature,\n  paginatedRowModel: createPaginatedRowModel(), // if using client-side pagination\n  // manualPagination: true, // if using manual server-side pagination\n})\n\nconst table = useTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n})\n```\n\n## Pagination (Octane) Guide\n\nTanStack Table has great support for both client-side and server-side pagination. This guide will walk you through the different ways to implement pagination in your table.\n\n### Client-Side Pagination\n\nUsing client-side pagination means that the `data` that you fetch will contain **_ALL_** of the rows for the table, and the table instance will handle pagination logic in the front-end.\n\n#### Should You Use Client-Side Pagination?\n\nClient-side pagination is usually the simplest option when the browser can fetch and retain the complete dataset. Use server-side pagination when the full dataset would be too expensive to query, transfer, or store in the browser.\n\nRow count alone does not decide the boundary. See the [Client-Side vs Server-Side Guide](../../../guide/client-side-vs-server-side) for the full decision framework, performance factors, and guidance for keeping filtering and sorting consistent with pagination.\n\n#### Should You Use Virtualization Instead?\n\nVirtualization (or windowing) reduces rendering work by mounting only the visible rows, but the virtualized data still exists in the browser. It can complement client-side or server-side pagination, but it does not replace server-side processing when the complete dataset is too large to load.\n\nSee the [Client-Side vs Server-Side Guide](../../../guide/client-side-vs-server-side#rendering-is-a-separate-decision) for that distinction, or [TanStack Virtual](https://tanstack.com/virtual/latest) for virtualization APIs.\n\n#### Pagination Row Model\n\nIf you want to take advantage of the built-in client-side pagination in TanStack Table, add the `rowPaginationFeature` and the `paginatedRowModel` factory to your `tableFeatures` call:\n\n```tsx\nimport {\n  useTable,\n  tableFeatures,\n  rowPaginationFeature,\n  createPaginatedRowModel,\n} from '@tanstack/octane-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  rowPaginationFeature,\n  paginatedRowModel: createPaginatedRowModel(),\n})\n\nconst table = useTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n})\n```\n\n### Manual Server-Side Pagination\n\nIf you decide that you need to use server-side pagination, here is how you can implement it.\n\nNo pagination row model is needed for server-side pagination, but if you have provided it for other tables that do need it in a shared component, you can still turn off the client-side pagination by setting the `manualPagination` option to `true`. Setting the `manualPagination` option to `true` will tell the table instance to use the `table.getPrePaginatedRowModel` row model under the hood, and it will make the table instance assume that the `data` that you pass in is already paginated.\n\n#### Page Count and Row Count\n\nThe table instance will have no way of knowing how many rows/pages there are in total in your back-end unless you tell it. Provide either the `rowCount` or `pageCount` table option to let the table instance know how many pages there are in total. If you provide a `rowCount`, the table instance will calculate the `pageCount` internally from `rowCount` and `pageSize`. Otherwise, you can directly provide the `pageCount` if you already have it. If you don't know the page count, pass `-1` for `pageCount`. In that case, `getCanNextPage()` returns `true` because the table cannot detect the end, `getCanPreviousPage()` depends on the current `pageIndex`, and `getCanLastPage()` returns `false` because no finite last page is known.\n\n```tsx\nimport {\n  useTable,\n  tableFeatures,\n  rowPaginationFeature,\n} from '@tanstack/octane-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({ rowPaginationFeature }) // no paginatedRowModel for server-side pagination\n\nconst table = useTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n  manualPagination: true, // turn off client-side pagination\n  rowCount: dataQuery.data?.rowCount, // pass in the total row count so the table knows how many pages there are (pageCount calculated internally if not provided)\n  // pageCount: dataQuery.data?.pageCount, // alternatively directly pass in pageCount instead of rowCount\n})\n```\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> Setting the `manualPagination` option to `true` will make the table instance assume that the `data` that you pass in is already paginated.\n\n### Pagination State\n\nWhether or not you are using client-side or manual server-side pagination, you can use the built-in `pagination` state and APIs.\n\nThe `pagination` state is an object that contains the following properties:\n\n- `pageIndex`: The current page index (zero-based).\n- `pageSize`: The current page size.\n\nFor reactive reads that should re-render your UI, use `table.state.pagination`. In event handlers, you can read the current snapshot with `table.atoms.pagination.get()`, but this read does not subscribe the component to future changes.\n\nIf you need access to the `pagination` state outside of the table (a server-side query key is the most common case), you can own the slice yourself. The recommended way in v9 is an external atom passed through the `atoms` table option. Atoms preserve fine-grained subscriptions, and the pagination value can be used in a query key without forcing the component that owns the table to re-render.\n\n```tsx\nimport { useCreateAtom, useSelector } from '@tanstack/octane-store'\nimport {\n  useTable,\n  tableFeatures,\n  rowPaginationFeature,\n  createPaginatedRowModel,\n  type PaginationState,\n} from '@tanstack/octane-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  rowPaginationFeature,\n  paginatedRowModel: createPaginatedRowModel(),\n})\n\nconst paginationAtom = useCreateAtom<PaginationState>({\n  pageIndex: 0, // initial page index\n  pageSize: 10, // default page size\n})\n\n// subscribe to the atom wherever you need the value (e.g. for a query key)\nconst pagination = useSelector(paginationAtom)\n\nconst table = useTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n  atoms: {\n    pagination: paginationAtom, // table pagination APIs now update paginationAtom\n  },\n})\n```\n\nAlternatively, the v8-style `state.pagination` plus `onPaginationChange` pattern is still supported. It can be convenient for simple integrations or when migrating v8 code, but it is less fine-grained than external atoms. See the [Table State Guide](./table-state) for a deeper comparison.\n\n```tsx\nconst [pagination, setPagination] = useState<PaginationState>({\n  pageIndex: 0, // initial page index\n  pageSize: 10, // default page size\n})\n\nconst table = useTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n  onPaginationChange: setPagination,\n  state: {\n    pagination,\n  },\n})\n```\n\nAlternatively, if you have no need for managing the `pagination` state in your own scope, but you need to set different initial values for the `pageIndex` and `pageSize`, you can use the `initialState` option.\n\n```tsx\nconst table = useTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n  initialState: {\n    pagination: {\n      pageIndex: 2, // custom initial page index\n      pageSize: 25, // custom default page size\n    },\n  },\n})\n```\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> Do NOT provide the `pagination` slice in more than one of the `atoms`, `state`, and `initialState` options. Controlled values (`atoms` or `state`) will overwrite `initialState`. Only use one of them.\n\n### Pagination Options\n\nBesides the `manualPagination`, `pageCount`, and `rowCount` options which are useful for manual server-side pagination (and discussed [above](#manual-server-side-pagination)), there is one other table option that is useful to understand.\n\n#### Auto Reset Page Index\n\nBy default, `pageIndex` is reset to `0` whenever the client-side row models recompute, such as when the `data` is updated, filters change, sorting changes, or grouping changes. This behavior is automatically disabled when `manualPagination` is `true`, but it can be overridden by explicitly assigning a boolean value to the `autoResetPageIndex` table option. There is also a global `autoResetAll` table option that disables (or enables) every auto-reset behavior at once.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> Automatic resets run only when an included client-side row model that triggers them recomputes. If a manual server-side table omits the filtered, sorted, grouped, or other relevant row model, changing that controlled state does not trigger a page-index reset, even when `autoResetPageIndex` or `autoResetAll` is `true`. Reset `pageIndex` yourself in the corresponding change handler.\n\n```tsx\nconst table = useTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n  autoResetPageIndex: false, // turn off auto reset of pageIndex\n  // autoResetAll: false, // or turn off all auto resets at once\n})\n```\n\nA common reason to set `autoResetPageIndex: false` is editing data while viewing the table (for example, inline cell editing). Every edit updates `data`, which recomputes the row models and would otherwise snap the user back to the first page. Setting the option to a static `false` keeps the current page when the row model recomputes. If you also use the expanding feature, pair it with `autoResetExpanded: false` so expanded rows do not collapse on edits.\n\nBe aware, however, that if you turn off `autoResetPageIndex`, you may need to add some logic to handle resetting the `pageIndex` yourself to avoid showing empty pages.\n\n### Pagination APIs\n\nThere are several pagination table instance APIs that are useful for hooking up your pagination UI components.\n\n#### Pagination Button APIs\n\n- `getCanPreviousPage`: Useful for disabling the \"previous page\" button when on the first page.\n- `getCanNextPage`: Useful for disabling the \"next page\" button when there are no more pages.\n- `getCanLastPage`: Useful for disabling the \"last page\" button when no finite last page is known.\n- `previousPage`: Useful for going to the previous page. (Button click handler)\n- `nextPage`: Useful for going to the next page. (Button click handler)\n- `firstPage`: Useful for going to the first page. (Button click handler)\n- `lastPage`: Useful for going to the last page. (Button click handler)\n- `setPageIndex`: Useful for a \"go to page\" input.\n- `resetPageIndex`: Useful for resetting the table state to the original page index.\n- `setPageSize`: Useful for a \"page size\" input/select.\n- `resetPageSize`: Useful for resetting the table state to the original page size.\n- `setPagination`: Useful for setting all of the pagination state at once.\n- `resetPagination`: Useful for resetting the table state to the original pagination state.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> These pagination APIs are available when using `rowPaginationFeature`.\n\n```tsx\n<Button\n  onClick={() => table.firstPage()}\n  disabled={!table.getCanPreviousPage()}\n>\n  {'<<'}\n</Button>\n<Button\n  onClick={() => table.previousPage()}\n  disabled={!table.getCanPreviousPage()}\n>\n  {'<'}\n</Button>\n<Button\n  onClick={() => table.nextPage()}\n  disabled={!table.getCanNextPage()}\n>\n  {'>'}\n</Button>\n<Button\n  onClick={() => table.lastPage()}\n  disabled={!table.getCanLastPage()}\n>\n  {'>>'}\n</Button>\n<select\n  value={table.state.pagination.pageSize}\n  onChange={e => {\n    table.setPageSize(Number(e.target.value))\n  }}\n>\n  {[10, 20, 30, 40, 50].map(pageSize => (\n    <option key={pageSize} value={pageSize}>\n      {pageSize}\n    </option>\n  ))}\n</select>\n```\n\n#### Pagination Info APIs\n\n- `getPageCount`: Useful for showing the total number of pages.\n- `getRowCount`: Useful for showing the total number of rows.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Pagination (Octane) Guide\n---\n\n## Examples\n\nWant to skip to the implementation? Check out these Octane examples:\n\n- [Pagination](../examples/pagination)\n\n### Pagination Setup\n\nHere's how you set up your table to use pagination features. Adding the pagination feature enables the related APIs. If you use client-side pagination, also set up `paginatedRowModel` after its feature, since row model slots are type-checked.\n\n```tsx\nimport {\n  useTable,\n  tableFeatures,\n  rowPaginationFeature,\n  createPaginatedRowModel,\n} from '@tanstack/octane-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  rowPaginationFeature,\n  paginatedRowModel: createPaginatedRowModel(), // if using client-side pagination\n  // manualPagination: true, // if using manual server-side pagination\n})\n\nconst table = useTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n})\n```\n\n## Pagination (Octane) Guide\n\nTanStack Table has great support for both client-side and server-side pagination. This guide will walk you through the different ways to implement pagination in your table.\n\n### Client-Side Pagination\n\nUsing client-side pagination means that the `data` that you fetch will contain **_ALL_** of the rows for the table, and the table instance will handle pagination logic in the front-end.\n\n#### Should You Use Client-Side Pagination?\n\nClient-side pagination is usually the simplest option when the browser can fetch and retain the complete dataset. Use server-side pagination when the full dataset would be too expensive to query, transfer, or store in the browser.\n\nRow count alone does not decide the boundary. See the [Client-Side vs Server-Side Guide](../../../guide/client-side-vs-server-side) for the full decision framework, performance factors, and guidance for keeping filtering and sorting consistent with pagination.\n\n#### Should You Use Virtualization Instead?\n\nVirtualization (or windowing) reduces rendering work by mounting only the visible rows, but the virtualized data still exists in the browser. It can complement client-side or server-side pagination, but it does not replace server-side processing when the complete dataset is too large to load.\n\nSee the [Client-Side vs Server-Side Guide](../../../guide/client-side-vs-server-side#rendering-is-a-separate-decision) for that distinction, or [TanStack Virtual](https://tanstack.com/virtual/latest) for virtualization APIs.\n\n#### Pagination Row Model\n\nIf you want to take advantage of the built-in client-side pagination in TanStack Table, add the `rowPaginationFeature` and the `paginatedRowModel` factory to your `tableFeatures` call:\n\n```tsx\nimport {\n  useTable,\n  tableFeatures,\n  rowPaginationFeature,\n  createPaginatedRowModel,\n} from '@tanstack/octane-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  rowPaginationFeature,\n  paginatedRowModel: createPaginatedRowModel(),\n})\n\nconst table = useTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n})\n```\n\n### Manual Server-Side Pagination\n\nIf you decide that you need to use server-side pagination, here is how you can implement it.\n\nNo pagination row model is needed for server-side pagination, but if you have provided it for other tables that do need it in a shared component, you can still turn off the client-side pagination by setting the `manualPagination` option to `true`. Setting the `manualPagination` option to `true` will tell the table instance to use the `table.getPrePaginatedRowModel` row model under the hood, and it will make the table instance assume that the `data` that you pass in is already paginated.\n\n#### Page Count and Row Count\n\nThe table instance will have no way of knowing how many rows/pages there are in total in your back-end unless you tell it. Provide either the `rowCount` or `pageCount` table option to let the table instance know how many pages there are in total. If you provide a `rowCount`, the table instance will calculate the `pageCount` internally from `rowCount` and `pageSize`. Otherwise, you can directly provide the `pageCount` if you already have it. If you don't know the page count, pass `-1` for `pageCount`. In that case, `getCanNextPage()` returns `true` because the table cannot detect the end, `getCanPreviousPage()` depends on the current `pageIndex`, and `getCanLastPage()` returns `false` because no finite last page is known.\n\n```tsx\nimport {\n  useTable,\n  tableFeatures,\n  rowPaginationFeature,\n} from '@tanstack/octane-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({ rowPaginationFeature }) // no paginatedRowModel for server-side pagination\n\nconst table = useTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n  manualPagination: true, // turn off client-side pagination\n  rowCount: dataQuery.data?.rowCount, // pass in the total row count so the table knows how many pages there are (pageCount calculated internally if not provided)\n  // pageCount: dataQuery.data?.pageCount, // alternatively directly pass in pageCount instead of rowCount\n})\n```\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> Setting the `manualPagination` option to `true` will make the table instance assume that the `data` that you pass in is already paginated.\n\n### Pagination State\n\nWhether or not you are using client-side or manual server-side pagination, you can use the built-in `pagination` state and APIs.\n\nThe `pagination` state is an object that contains the following properties:\n\n- `pageIndex`: The current page index (zero-based).\n- `pageSize`: The current page size.\n\nFor reactive reads that should re-render your UI, use `table.state.pagination`. In event handlers, you can read the current snapshot with `table.atoms.pagination.get()`, but this read does not subscribe the component to future changes.\n\nIf you need access to the `pagination` state outside of the table (a server-side query key is the most common case), you can own the slice yourself. The recommended way in v9 is an external atom passed through the `atoms` table option. Atoms preserve fine-grained subscriptions, and the pagination value can be used in a query key without forcing the component that owns the table to re-render.\n\n```tsx\nimport { useCreateAtom, useSelector } from '@tanstack/octane-store'\nimport {\n  useTable,\n  tableFeatures,\n  rowPaginationFeature,\n  createPaginatedRowModel,\n  type PaginationState,\n} from '@tanstack/octane-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  rowPaginationFeature,\n  paginatedRowModel: createPaginatedRowModel(),\n})\n\nconst paginationAtom = useCreateAtom<PaginationState>({\n  pageIndex: 0, // initial page index\n  pageSize: 10, // default page size\n})\n\n// subscribe to the atom wherever you need the value (e.g. for a query key)\nconst pagination = useSelector(paginationAtom)\n\nconst table = useTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n  atoms: {\n    pagination: paginationAtom, // table pagination APIs now update paginationAtom\n  },\n})\n```\n\nAlternatively, the v8-style `state.pagination` plus `onPaginationChange` pattern is still supported. It can be convenient for simple integrations or when migrating v8 code, but it is less fine-grained than external atoms. See the [Table State Guide](./table-state) for a deeper comparison.\n\n```tsx\nconst [pagination, setPagination] = useState<PaginationState>({\n  pageIndex: 0, // initial page index\n  pageSize: 10, // default page size\n})\n\nconst table = useTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n  onPaginationChange: setPagination,\n  state: {\n    pagination,\n  },\n})\n```\n\nAlternatively, if you have no need for managing the `pagination` state in your own scope, but you need to set different initial values for the `pageIndex` and `pageSize`, you can use the `initialState` option.\n\n```tsx\nconst table = useTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n  initialState: {\n    pagination: {\n      pageIndex: 2, // custom initial page index\n      pageSize: 25, // custom default page size\n    },\n  },\n})\n```\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> Do NOT provide the `pagination` slice in more than one of the `atoms`, `state`, and `initialState` options. Controlled values (`atoms` or `state`) will overwrite `initialState`. Only use one of them.\n\n### Pagination Options\n\nBesides the `manualPagination`, `pageCount`, and `rowCount` options which are useful for manual server-side pagination (and discussed [above](#manual-server-side-pagination)), there is one other table option that is useful to understand.\n\n#### Auto Reset Page Index\n\nBy default, `pageIndex` is reset to `0` whenever the client-side row models recompute, such as when the `data` is updated, filters change, sorting changes, or grouping changes. This behavior is automatically disabled when `manualPagination` is `true`, but it can be overridden by explicitly assigning a boolean value to the `autoResetPageIndex` table option. There is also a global `autoResetAll` table option that disables (or enables) every auto-reset behavior at once.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> Automatic resets run only when an included client-side row model that triggers them recomputes. If a manual server-side table omits the filtered, sorted, grouped, or other relevant row model, changing that controlled state does not trigger a page-index reset, even when `autoResetPageIndex` or `autoResetAll` is `true`. Reset `pageIndex` yourself in the corresponding change handler.\n\n```tsx\nconst table = useTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n  autoResetPageIndex: false, // turn off auto reset of pageIndex\n  // autoResetAll: false, // or turn off all auto resets at once\n})\n```\n\nA common reason to set `autoResetPageIndex: false` is editing data while viewing the table (for example, inline cell editing). Every edit updates `data`, which recomputes the row models and would otherwise snap the user back to the first page. Setting the option to a static `false` keeps the current page when the row model recomputes. If you also use the expanding feature, pair it with `autoResetExpanded: false` so expanded rows do not collapse on edits.\n\nBe aware, however, that if you turn off `autoResetPageIndex`, you may need to add some logic to handle resetting the `pageIndex` yourself to avoid showing empty pages.\n\n### Pagination APIs\n\nThere are several pagination table instance APIs that are useful for hooking up your pagination UI components.\n\n#### Pagination Button APIs\n\n- `getCanPreviousPage`: Useful for disabling the \"previous page\" button when on the first page.\n- `getCanNextPage`: Useful for disabling the \"next page\" button when there are no more pages.\n- `getCanLastPage`: Useful for disabling the \"last page\" button when no finite last page is known.\n- `previousPage`: Useful for going to the previous page. (Button click handler)\n- `nextPage`: Useful for going to the next page. (Button click handler)\n- `firstPage`: Useful for going to the first page. (Button click handler)\n- `lastPage`: Useful for going to the last page. (Button click handler)\n- `setPageIndex`: Useful for a \"go to page\" input.\n- `resetPageIndex`: Useful for resetting the table state to the original page index.\n- `setPageSize`: Useful for a \"page size\" input/select.\n- `resetPageSize`: Useful for resetting the table state to the original page size.\n- `setPagination`: Useful for setting all of the pagination state at once.\n- `resetPagination`: Useful for resetting the table state to the original pagination state.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> These pagination APIs are available when using `rowPaginationFeature`.\n\n```tsx\n<Button\n  onClick={() => table.firstPage()}\n  disabled={!table.getCanPreviousPage()}\n>\n  {'<<'}\n</Button>\n<Button\n  onClick={() => table.previousPage()}\n  disabled={!table.getCanPreviousPage()}\n>\n  {'<'}\n</Button>\n<Button\n  onClick={() => table.nextPage()}\n  disabled={!table.getCanNextPage()}\n>\n  {'>'}\n</Button>\n<Button\n  onClick={() => table.lastPage()}\n  disabled={!table.getCanLastPage()}\n>\n  {'>>'}\n</Button>\n<select\n  value={table.state.pagination.pageSize}\n  onChange={e => {\n    table.setPageSize(Number(e.target.value))\n  }}\n>\n  {[10, 20, 30, 40, 50].map(pageSize => (\n    <option key={pageSize} value={pageSize}>\n      {pageSize}\n    </option>\n  ))}\n</select>\n```\n\n#### Pagination Info APIs\n\n- `getPageCount`: Useful for showing the total number of pages.\n- `getRowCount`: Useful for showing the total number of rows.\n"}
